\title{Escher Staircases and Separated Filtrations:\\Infinite Ideal Chains in Polynomial and Function Rings}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{July 17, 2026}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
We examine an ``Escher staircase'' metaphor for infinite chains of ideals and separate three phenomena that the metaphor can obscure: strict ascending chains, automatic intersection identities, and separated descending filtrations. An Escher staircase is an infinite strictly ascending chain of ideals. Such a chain exists in a commutative ring exactly when the ring is non-Noetherian. We construct an explicit staircase in the countably generated polynomial ring $k[x_0,x_1,\ldots]$ over a field $k$, using $V_n=(x_0,\ldots,x_{n-1})$. Strictness follows from a missing-variable lemma proved by a substitution endomorphism. In contrast, Hilbert's basis theorem excludes every infinite strict ascending ideal chain from $k[x_0,\ldots,x_{m-1}]$ for finite $m$. We also correct a proposed powers-of-two construction. For integer-valued functions, the ideals $D_n=\{f:2^n\mid f(z)\text{ for all }z\}$ form a strict descending, not ascending, chain, and their intersection is zero. These results identify a sharp finite/infinite variable dichotomy, distinguish arbitrary ideal ascent from Krull dimension, and motivate associated graded objects as the appropriate setting for divisibility-based invariants.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}

Ideal chains measure how algebraic constraints accumulate. An ascending chain
\[
 I_0\subseteq I_1\subseteq I_2\subseteq\cdots
\]
represents successive enlargement, whereas a descending chain represents successive refinement. The diagrams differ by one arrow direction, but their meanings are fundamentally different.

We use the term \emph{Escher staircase} for an infinite strictly ascending chain of ideals. Strictness is the substantive feature. If an ascending chain starts at $I_0$, then $I_0$ is automatically contained in every rung and
\[
 \bigcap_{n\geq 0}I_n=I_0.
\]
Thus an intersection that returns to the first rung is not paradoxical. Likewise, zero belongs to every ideal, so common membership of zero imposes no extra condition.

Our first objective is a genuine explicit staircase. For a field $k$, set
\[
 R_\infty=k[x_0,x_1,x_2,\ldots],\qquad
 V_n=(x_0,\ldots,x_{n-1}),\quad V_0=(0).
\]
Then $V_0\subsetneq V_1\subsetneq\cdots$. A substitution map annihilating the preceding variables while fixing $x_n$ proves that $x_n\notin V_n$.

Our second objective is the boundary of this construction. Hilbert's basis theorem makes every finite-variable ring $k[x_0,\ldots,x_{m-1}]$ Noetherian, so no infinite strict ascending chain exists. Countably many variables cross a sharp threshold that no finite number crosses.

Our third objective is corrective. In the pointwise function ring $\Z^\Z$, the ideals
\[
 D_n=\{f:\Z\to\Z:f(z)\in2^n\Z\text{ for every }z\in\Z\}
\]
satisfy $D_{n+1}\subsetneq D_n$ and $\bigcap_nD_n=(0)$. They form a strict separated filtration, not an Escher staircase.

\section{Definitions and general structure}

Throughout, rings are commutative with identity.

\begin{definition}[Ideal]
An ideal $I$ of a ring $R$ is an additive subgroup of $R$ such that $ri\in I$ for every $r\in R$ and $i\in I$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Escher staircase]
An Escher staircase in $R$ is a sequence of ideals $(I_n)_{n\geq0}$ satisfying $I_n\subsetneq I_{n+1}$ for every $n\geq0$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Noetherian ring]
A ring $R$ is Noetherian if every ideal is finitely generated. Equivalently, every ascending chain of ideals stabilizes.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Separated filtration]
A descending filtration by ideals is a sequence $(F_n)_{n\geq0}$ with $F_{n+1}\subseteq F_n$. It is strict if every inclusion is strict and separated if $\bigcap_{n\geq0}F_n=(0)$.
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}[Intersection of an ascending chain]
If $I_0\subseteq I_1\subseteq I_2\subseteq\cdots$, then
\[
 \bigcap_{n\geq0}I_n=I_0.
\]
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
The first ideal is contained in every later ideal, hence in the intersection. The intersection is contained in each family member, in particular in $I_0$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Noetherian characterization by staircases]
A commutative ring $R$ admits an Escher staircase if and only if $R$ is not Noetherian.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
A staircase never stabilizes and therefore contradicts the ascending chain condition. Conversely, if $R$ is not Noetherian, some ideal $I$ is not finitely generated. Choose $a_0\in I$. Once $a_0,\ldots,a_{n-1}$ have been selected, their generated ideal cannot equal $I$, so choose
\[
 a_n\in I\setminus(a_0,\ldots,a_{n-1}).
\]
Then
\[
 (a_0)\subsetneq(a_0,a_1)\subsetneq(a_0,a_1,a_2)\subsetneq\cdots
\]
is an Escher staircase.
\end{proof}

\section{The powers-of-two filtration}

Let $A=\Z^\Z$ with pointwise addition and multiplication, and define
\[
 D_n=\{f\in A:\forall z\in\Z,\;2^n\mid f(z)\}.
\]

\begin{proposition}[Ideal property]
For every $n\geq0$, $D_n$ is an ideal of $A$.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
The zero function belongs to $D_n$. Pointwise divisibility by $2^n$ is preserved under addition and under multiplication by an arbitrary integer-valued function.
\end{proof}

\begin{proposition}[Reversed containment]
For every $n\geq0$, $D_{n+1}\subseteq D_n$.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
Divisibility by $2^{n+1}$ implies divisibility by $2^n$, point by point.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Strict descent]
For every $n\geq0$, $D_{n+1}\subsetneq D_n$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
The constant function $c_n(z)=2^n$ belongs to $D_n$ but not to $D_{n+1}$, since $2^{n+1}$ does not divide $2^n$.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Unbounded powers force vanishing]
If $a\in\Z$ and $2^n\mid a$ for every $n\geq0$, then $a=0$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
If $a\neq0$, choose $n$ with $2^n>|a|$. Divisibility gives $a=2^nq$ for a nonzero integer $q$, and hence $|a|=2^n|q|\geq2^n$, a contradiction.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Separation theorem]
The powers-of-two filtration is separated:
\[
 \bigcap_{n\geq0}D_n=(0).
\]
Equivalently, a function belongs to every $D_n$ exactly when it is the zero function.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
If $f$ lies in every $D_n$, then for each fixed $z$, every power of two divides $f(z)$. The preceding lemma gives $f(z)=0$. Thus $f=0$, and the reverse inclusion is immediate.
\end{proof}

\subsection{Integer-valued polynomials}

The ring of integer-valued polynomials is
\[
 \IntZ=\{f\in\mathbb Q[x]:f(z)\in\Z\text{ for every }z\in\Z\}.
\]
Define
\[
 E_n=\{f\in\IntZ:f(\Z)\subseteq2^n\Z\}.
\]
Then $E_{n+1}\subseteq E_n$. The constant polynomial $2^n$ proves strictness, and evaluation at every integer proves separation. This family is therefore a descending arithmetic filtration. Its associated graded object
\[
 \bigoplus_{n\geq0}E_n/E_{n+1}
\]
is a natural target for further study.

\section{The countably infinite polynomial staircase}

Let $R_\infty=k[x_0,x_1,x_2,\ldots]$. Each polynomial involves finitely many variables, although no fixed finite variable set contains the entire ring.

\begin{lemma}[Missing-variable lemma]
Let $S\subseteq\N$ and $j\notin S$. Then
\[
 x_j\notin(x_i:i\in S).
\]
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Define a $k$-algebra endomorphism $\varphi_S:R_\infty\to R_\infty$ by
\[
 \varphi_S(x_i)=
 \begin{cases}
 0,&i\in S,\\
 x_i,&i\notin S.
 \end{cases}
\]
It annihilates every generator $x_i$ for $i\in S$, so the generated ideal lies in its kernel. If $x_j$ belonged to that ideal, then $\varphi_S(x_j)=0$. But $j\notin S$, so $\varphi_S(x_j)=x_j\neq0$, a contradiction.
\end{proof}

\begin{definition}[Variable ideals]
For $n\geq0$, let
\[
 V_n=(x_0,x_1,\ldots,x_{n-1}),
\]
where $V_0=(0)$.
\end{definition}

\begin{proposition}[Monotonicity and strictness witnesses]
For every $n\geq0$, $V_n\subseteq V_{n+1}$, $x_n\in V_{n+1}$, and $x_n\notin V_n$.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
The generators of $V_n$ occur among those of $V_{n+1}$, and $x_n$ is an added generator. Apply the missing-variable lemma to $S=\{0,\ldots,n-1\}$ for non-membership.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Infinite polynomial staircase]
The sequence $(V_n)_{n\geq0}$ is an Escher staircase:
\[
 V_0\subsetneq V_1\subsetneq V_2\subsetneq\cdots.
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
The preceding proposition supplies inclusion and the witness $x_n$ proving strictness at every step.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Non-Noetherianity]
The ring $k[x_0,x_1,x_2,\ldots]$ is not Noetherian.
\end{corollary}
\begin{proof}
Its variable-ideal chain does not stabilize, contrary to the ascending chain condition.
\end{proof}

\begin{proposition}[Intersection of the variable staircase]
One has
\[
 \bigcap_{n\geq0}V_n=(0).
\]
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
The family contains $V_0=(0)$, so its intersection is contained in zero; the reverse inclusion is universal for ideals.
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}
The intersection identity is not an additional looping phenomenon. It is automatic for an ascending chain beginning at zero. The essential phenomenon is that strict ascent continues indefinitely.
\end{remark}

\section{The finite-variable obstruction}

Let $R_m=k[x_0,\ldots,x_{m-1}]$ for a finite nonnegative integer $m$.

\begin{theorem}[Finite-variable exclusion]
For every field $k$ and every finite $m$, the polynomial ring $R_m$ admits no Escher staircase.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
A field is Noetherian. Hilbert's basis theorem says that adjoining one polynomial variable preserves Noetherianity. Iterating it $m$ times shows that $R_m$ is Noetherian, so every ascending ideal chain stabilizes.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[One-variable case]
The polynomial ring $k[x]$ has no Escher staircase.
\end{corollary}
\begin{proof}
Take $m=1$ in the finite-variable exclusion theorem. Alternatively, $k[x]$ is a principal ideal domain and hence Noetherian.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Finite/countable dichotomy]
Polynomial rings over a field satisfy the following sharp dichotomy:
\begin{enumerate}[label=(\roman*)]
\item $k[x_0,\ldots,x_{m-1}]$ has no Escher staircase for every finite $m$;
\item $k[x_0,x_1,x_2,\ldots]$ has the explicit staircase $V_n=(x_0,\ldots,x_{n-1})$.
\end{enumerate}
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Combine the infinite polynomial staircase theorem with the finite-variable exclusion theorem.
\end{proof}

The theorem is stronger than observing that a coordinate chain exhausts finitely many named variables. Hilbert's basis theorem controls every ascending chain, including chains generated by complicated non-coordinate polynomials.

\section{Krull dimension and valuation depth}

Krull dimension uses chains of prime ideals. The ring $k[x_1,\ldots,x_m]$ has Krull dimension $m$, but this does not conflict with the absence of an infinite staircase. Dimension-witnessing chains are finite and prime; an Escher staircase is countably infinite and permits arbitrary ideals.

Thus a proposed staircase height cannot equal $m$ when it means the length of an infinite ascending chain. Every finite-variable polynomial ring has no such chain. Dimension-sensitive information is better sought in a descending filtration. For a Noetherian local ring $(R,\mathfrak m)$, the eventual polynomial growth of
\[
 \ell(R/\mathfrak m^n)
\]
has degree $\dim R$. Hilbert--Samuel growth links dimension to quantitative filtration geometry.

Discrete valuation rings offer another contrast. Their ideals are arranged by powers of one maximal ideal,
\[
 R\supsetneq\mathfrak m\supsetneq\mathfrak m^2\supsetneq\cdots.
\]
They are Noetherian, so they have no Escher staircase, despite having an infinite strict descending valuation filtration. In particular, the $p$-adic integers $\Z_p$ exhibit descending depth but no infinite strict ascending ideal chain.

\section{Algorithms and numerical demonstrations}

\begin{algorithm}[Divisibility-level classifier]
Given integers $a_1,\ldots,a_r$ and a maximum level $N$, test for each $0\leq n\leq N$ whether $2^n$ divides every $a_i$. Once a level fails, all higher levels fail. Direct modular testing uses $O(rN)$ arithmetic operations. Computing the individual $2$-adic valuations first gives $O(r+N)$ arithmetic operations apart from bit complexity.
\end{algorithm}

\begin{algorithm}[Variable-ideal membership]
For $V_n=(x_0,\ldots,x_{n-1})$, a monomial belongs to $V_n$ exactly when it is divisible by at least one of $x_0,\ldots,x_{n-1}$. A sparse polynomial belongs to $V_n$ exactly when every supported monomial passes this test. Scanning exponent dictionaries takes time linear in the number of stored variable occurrences.
\end{algorithm}

\begin{algorithm}[Coordinate-chain monitor]
For a finite pool of $m$ variables, form $W_n=(x_0,\ldots,x_{\min(n,m)-1})$. The chain is strict for $n<m$ and constant for $n\geq m$. For an unbounded variable supply, each stage has the new witness $x_n$. This finite simulation illustrates, but does not replace, the universal Noetherian theorem.
\end{algorithm}

These computations serve as transparent witnesses. The constant function $2^n$ displays strict descent, and the variable $x_n$ displays strict ascent. Neither requires searching for a complicated counterexample.

\section{Applications and interpretation}

\subsection{Diagnosing non-Noetherianity}
An explicit staircase certifies that a ring is non-Noetherian. The variable staircase is especially useful because every strictness witness is canonical. Similar constructions apply whenever infinitely many algebraic directions escape all finite predecessors.

\subsection{Termination of ideal processes}
Noetherianity underlies termination arguments. If each iteration strictly enlarges an ideal, a Noetherian ambient ring permits only finitely many iterations. Procedures transferred to infinitely generated rings therefore require separate termination arguments.

\subsection{Filtrations as resolution scales}
The ideals $D_n$ encode binary congruence to precision $n$. The quotient $D_n/D_{n+1}$ isolates the layer with exactly one additional power of two. The intersection records only separation, whereas all graded layers together can retain arithmetic structure.

\subsection{Limits of the metaphor}
The Escher image should not be attached to the intersection identity, which is automatic, or to divisibility thresholds, which point downward. Used precisely, it describes endless acquisition of new generators.

\section{Discussion}

The results give a conceptual taxonomy:
\begin{itemize}
\item infinite strict ascent detects failure of Noetherianity;
\item strict separated descent detects increasing arithmetic resolution;
\item Krull dimension measures prime-chain geometry, not the ordinal length of arbitrary ideal ascent.
\end{itemize}

The missing-variable proof illustrates a reusable method. To show that a candidate is not in an ideal generated by a set, construct a homomorphism that kills every generator while preserving the candidate. In the present setting, the universal substitution property of polynomial rings produces exactly that detector.

The divisibility filtration gives a methodological lesson of equal importance: check containment with explicit witnesses before defining invariants from a chain. The constant function $2^n$ proves both membership at level $n$ and non-membership at level $n+1$. The correction does not destroy the construction; it reveals its natural interpretation as a separated filtration.

\section{Future work}

For $\IntZ$, determine the associated graded ring $\bigoplus_nE_n/E_{n+1}$, preferably in terms of binomial polynomials. Strictness and separation isolate the remaining issue as arithmetic in the graded layers.

A second direction is the \emph{ordinal staircase spectrum}: the set of order types of strictly ascending well-ordered ideal chains. For $k[x_i:i<\kappa]$, coordinate ideals give lower bounds, while non-coordinate ideals may govern the full spectrum and its dependence on $|k|$.

Third, compare filtration growth with Krull dimension. In a Noetherian local domain, Hilbert--Samuel growth is the dimension-sensitive replacement for a forbidden infinite ascent.

Finally, study the ring of all algebraic integers through valuation filtrations. Fixing a rational prime $p$ and intersecting thresholds over primes above $p$ may yield separated filtrations whose graded pieces reflect ramification across finite extensions.

\section{Conclusion}

An Escher staircase is an infinite strictly ascending chain of ideals, and its existence is equivalent to non-Noetherianity. The ring $k[x_0,x_1,\ldots]$ has an explicit staircase generated by successive variables; substitution endomorphisms prove that every new variable creates a genuinely larger rung. Every finite-variable polynomial ring over a field lies on the opposite side of a sharp boundary because Hilbert's basis theorem rules out all infinite strict ascent.

Powers-of-two divisibility produces a different structure. Its ideals descend strictly and intersect in zero, yielding a separated filtration. Recognizing this reversal resolves the apparent paradox and directs attention toward associated graded rings, valuation layers, and asymptotic growth. Infinite coordinates permit endless ascent; finite generation prohibits it; divisibility leads inward, toward zero.

