# Explore Escher Staircases and Separated Filtrations

Begin with the central correction: stronger divisibility means a **smaller** ideal, while adding a genuinely new polynomial variable means a **larger** ideal. The two geometries are complementary, not contradictory.

## 1. Descend through binary resolution

Use the live explorer to enter integer samples and find the last common divisibility level.

{{interactive_demo:0}}

Run the corresponding numerical table and inspect the funnel visualization.

{{demo:0}}

{{visualization:1}}

<details><summary>Click to reveal the separation argument</summary>
If an integer is divisible by every $2^n$ and is nonzero, choose $n$ with $2^n$ larger than its absolute value. A nonzero multiple of $2^n$ cannot be that small. Applying the argument pointwise proves that the intersection of all function ideals is zero.
</details>

Learn more about [filtrations](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Filtered_algebra) and [$p$-adic valuation](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/P-adic_valuation).

## 2. Climb with fresh variables

Now reverse the geometry. At stage $n$, add $x_n$ to the generators.

{{interactive_demo:1}}

{{visualization:0}}

The sparse membership method makes the witness computationally explicit.

{{algorithm:1}}

{{demo:1}}

<details><summary>Click to reveal the missing-variable proof</summary>
To prove $x_n\notin(x_0,\ldots,x_{n-1})$, substitute zero for $x_0,\ldots,x_{n-1}$ and leave $x_n$ fixed. Every element generated by the earlier variables maps to zero, whereas $x_n$ remains nonzero.
</details>

## 3. Locate the finite/infinite boundary

A coordinate chain with finitely many variables visibly runs out of new coordinates. The deeper statement is Hilbert's basis theorem: **every** ascending chain of ideals stabilizes in a finite-variable polynomial ring over a field.

{{interactive_demo:2}}

{{visualization:2}}

{{demo:2}}

{{algorithm:2}}

<details><summary>Click to reveal why this is not Krull dimension</summary>
Krull dimension measures finite chains of prime ideals. An Escher staircase is an infinite chain of arbitrary ideals. Thus $k[x_1,\ldots,x_m]$ can have dimension $m$ while admitting no infinite strict ascending chain.
</details>

For background, see [Noetherian rings](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Noetherian_ring), [Hilbert's basis theorem](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hilbert%27s_basis_theorem), and [Krull dimension](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Krull_dimension).

## 4. Compute filtration depth efficiently

The minimum two-adic valuation algorithm avoids retesting every level independently.

{{algorithm:0}}

The resulting viewpoint suggests studying associated graded layers rather than treating a descending filtration as an ascending staircase.

